  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday Night through next Monday)\nIssued at 1149 AM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nA cold front will be working east through Middle TN Tuesday night\ninto Tuesday afternoon. The upper level trough will become a \nclosed off upper level low over the Great Lakes Tuesday night and\nthis will slow the cold front as it pushes east through Middle \nTN. This will bring a high chance for rain Tuesday night through \nTuesday morning with showers lingering over the eastern part of\nthe area into the early evening. Breezy winds will continue ahead\nof the cold front with gusts up 30 mph. The thunderstorm threat\ndoes look to remain low with this front with CAPE generally\nremaining low. The highest CAPE will be Wednesday afternoon from\nNashville east and may reach up to 300 j/kg. This is a dynamic low\nand there will be plenty of wind shear, if we end up seeing a\nlittle more CAPE we could get some gusty winds with any\nthunderstorms but at this point that looks very unlikely. This\nwill be another good soaking rain with rain totals generally \nranging from 1-2\". There may be some localized totals up to 3\"\nfrom Clarksville to Lafayette southwest to Lawrenceburg. \n\nUpper level flow will then turn more zonal for the rest of the\nweek. This will keep the weather quiet with highs in the upper 60s\nto lower 70s. A short wave trough and push of cold air will make \nit to the Great Lakes over the weekend. Some of that cold air will\nmake it here and will bring below normal highs with them falling \ninto the lower 60s. Overnight lows will also be cold falling into \nthe 30s and a frost will be likely starting Saturday night. Lows \nbelow 32 are also looking likely for the Plateau starting Saturday\nnight.",
